{:edb/db-before-t 2714 :edb/db-after-t 2715 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536027 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:12:12.482Z" 13194139536027 true] [17592186197987 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 true] [17592186197987 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\:\\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 false] [17592186197987 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 3\\cdot 1 = 3\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{y} &= ∬_(D)x^{2}λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}dxdy \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3}]_{0}^{3}dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{M}{3}∫_{1}^{2}9dy \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{1}^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3M(2 - 1) \\\\\n&= 3M\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} = 3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}\n$$" 13194139536027 false] [17592186197987 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nI_y = \\iint\\limits_D x^2\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $\\mathcal{A} =  3\\cdot 1 = 3.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x,y) = \\dfrac{M}{\\mathcal{A}} = \\dfrac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =3M[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3M(2-1) \\\\  & =3M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nR_y^2 =\\dfrac{3M}{M} =  3\\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad R_y = \\sqrt{3}.\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186197989 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies one-half of the circle $x^2+y^2\\leq 1$ for $y\\geq 0$. Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda(x,y)=\\dfrac{3}{2}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 false] [17592186197989 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies one-half of the circle $x^2+y^2\\leq 1$ for $y\\geq 0.$ Its mass density at any point is given by $\\lambda(x,y)=\\dfrac{3}{2}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 true] [17592186197989 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(r,\\theta)=\\dfrac{3}{2}Mr\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{0} &= ∬_(D)λ(r, θ)r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M∫_{0}^{π}∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\sin θdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M(∫_{0}^{1}r^{4}dr)(∫_{0}^{π}\\sin θdθ) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}r^{5} \\mid _{0}^{1} \\cdot (-\\cos θ) \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}M \\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{3M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{0} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{I_{0}}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{3M}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 false] [17592186197989 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(r,\\theta) r^3 \\:\\textrm{d}r\\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(r,\\theta)$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda(r,\\theta)=\\dfrac{3}{2}Mr\\sin\\theta.$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M({\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}M\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186197991 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y = x$, $x = 1$, and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 3Mx$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 false] [17592186197991 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the lines $y = x,$ $x = 1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda(x,y) = 3Mx.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 true] [17592186197991 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$, we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}}$,\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{0} &= ∬_(D)(x^{2} + y^{2})λ(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) \\cdot 3Mxdydx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}x^{3} + xy^{2}dydx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}[x^{3}y + \\frac{1}{3}xy^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = x}dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}x^{4} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}x^{4}dx \\\\\n&= 3M[\\frac{4}{15}x^{5}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 3M \\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4M}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{0} &= \\sqrt{\\frac{I_{0}}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5} \\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 false] [17592186197991 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_x$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n$R_x = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_x}{M}},$\nwhere $I_x$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_y$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n$R_y = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_y}{M}},$\nwhere $I_y$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration $R_0$ about the origin is given by\n$R_0 = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_0}{M}},$\nwhere $I_0$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2 + y^2) \\lambda(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda(x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 3Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}x{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3M{[\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3M\\cdot \\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186198344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379909 13194139536027 true] [17592186198344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379916 13194139536027 true] [17592186198344 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379923 13194139536027 true] [17592186198346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379930 13194139536027 true] [17592186198346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379951 13194139536027 true] [17592186198346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379958 13194139536027 true] [17592186198346 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379965 13194139536027 true] [17592186198348 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379937 13194139536027 true] [17592186198348 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379944 13194139536027 true] [17592186198348 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379972 13194139536027 true] [17592186198578 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536027 true] [17592186198578 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 1 \\le y \\le 3}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 false] [17592186198578 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the origin." 13194139536027 true] [17592186198578 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{8}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{[\\frac{4}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(4+9-(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}(13-\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{34}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{0}^{2}=\\frac{17}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186198591 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536027 true] [17592186198591 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y = x$, $x = 2$, and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{3}{4}My$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 false] [17592186198591 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the lines $y=x,$ $x=2,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{3}{4}My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 true] [17592186198591 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}{[\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{4}\\cdot \\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{12M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186198592 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536027 true] [17592186198592 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n\n$D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 4 \\le y \\le 6}$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 false] [17592186198592 1100 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,4\\le y\\le 6}.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139536027 true] [17592186198592 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 2=2.$ Since the plate has uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}[y]_{4}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(6-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{R}_{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⇒\\,{R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$" 13194139536027 true] [17592186198883 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536027 true] [17592186198883 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y = x^{2}$ and $x = y^{2}$. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = 12Mxy$. Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139536027 false] [17592186198883 1100 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $x={y}^{2}.$ Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=12Mxy.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139536027 true] [17592186198883 1145 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 12Mxy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\